 _Eigen Simulation_ is a `Java` application that lets you visualize three algorithms for finding _real eigenvectors_.
 
For starters, an eigenvector is a vector with the following property:
Let $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ be a matrix and $\mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ be a vector. Then $\mathbf{v}$ is a non-trivial solution (that is, $\mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0}$) of the equation below.

$$A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v}\;\;\;(1)$$

These eigenvectors and eigenvalues (the $\lambda$ values in (1)) are essential in *Mathematics* and very useful in fields such as *Physics*, *Optimization*, and *Machine Learning*. Here I explore three algorithms that compute a special kind of eigenvectors called *real eigenvectors*. For these *eigenvectors*, the matrix in (1) must satisfy $A = A^T$. These matrices are called [symmetric matrices](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetric_matrix), and they guarantee the existence of *real eigenvectors and eigenvalues*. The first method is the [power method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_iteration), and the other two are based on a constrained optimization problem:
$$\min_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n} \text{ or } \max_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n} \left < \mathbf{x}, A \mathbf{x}\right >$$
$$\text{such that  } \;\;|\mathbf{x}|^2 = 1 \;\;\; (2)$$

To visualize these algorithms, the application draws a sphere (the constraint space in (2)) painted with colors. The color represents the value of the cost function in (2). Brighter colors mean higher cost, while darker colors mean lower cost.

In the application, you can press the `3` key to see the gradient vector field of this cost function on the sphere. The video below shows a demo of this program.

Feel free to download the program below.

![_Eigen Simulation_ app video](https://youtu.be/0QV7QjFa0k8)

